<Base title="History" titleKey="meta.about" description="History and Sthalapurana of Sri Gopalakrishna Temple, Bilinele: the ascetic and Gurukula, Shukla Tirtha, Kudurekatte, and Go Durita Nivarana.">
  <article class="page history">
    <header class="page-intro wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow kicker" data-i18n="about.eyebrow">History & Sthalapurana</p>
      <h1 data-i18n="about.title">A sacred land of sages and spiritual tradition</h1>
      <p class="kannada" data-i18n="about.name">ಶ್ರೀ ಗೋಪಾಲಕೃಷ್ಣ ದೇವಸ್ಥಾನ, ಬಿಳಿನೆಲೆ</p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap">
      <section class="chapter">
        <figure class="chapter-media">
          <img src="/gallery/temple-side.png" alt="The temple from the side." data-i18n="gallery.exterior" data-i18n-attr="alt" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption data-i18n="gallery.exterior">The temple from the side.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 data-i18n="about.landTitle">The Sacred Land of Bilinele</h2>
          <p data-i18n="about.intro">
            Sri Gopalakrishna Temple is situated in Bilinele village of Kadaba Taluk, Dakshina Kannada, Karnataka. According to local tradition and the Sthalapurana, the spiritual heritage of this sacred kshetra extends back many centuries.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter flip">
        <figure class="chapter-media">
          <img src="/gallery/muni.png" alt="The Muni shrine." data-i18n="gallery.muni" data-i18n-attr="alt" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption data-i18n="gallery.muni">The Muni shrine.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 data-i18n="about.sagesTitle">The Sages</h2>
          <p data-i18n="about.ascetic">
            It is believed that, in ancient times, a great ascetic arrived at Bilinele accompanied by a community of disciples. Drawn by the natural beauty, peaceful surroundings and serene atmosphere of the place, the ascetic is said to have chosen this land for meditation and penance.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter">
        <figure class="chapter-media">
          <img src="/gallery/theertha-mantapa.png" alt="Theertha mantapa." data-i18n="gallery.mantapa" data-i18n-attr="alt" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption data-i18n="gallery.mantapa">Theertha mantapa.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 data-i18n="about.guruTitle">The Gurukula</h2>
          <p data-i18n="about.gurukula">
            A hermitage was established here, and the ascetic is believed to have imparted spiritual knowledge to his disciples through a traditional Gurukula. The disciples lived in this sacred environment, pursuing spiritual practices and learning under the guidance of their teacher.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter flip">
        <figure class="chapter-media">
          <img src="/gallery/pond.png" alt="The temple pond." data-i18n="gallery.pond" data-i18n-attr="alt" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption data-i18n="gallery.pond">The temple pond.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 data-i18n="about.tirthaTitle">Shukla Tirtha</h2>
          <p data-i18n="about.tirtha1">
            The stream flowing beside the temple is now known as the Bilinele Stream. According to local tradition, it was once known as Shukla Tirtha.
          </p>
          <p data-i18n="about.tirtha2">
            The Sthalapurana associates this sacred waterway and the surrounding land with the spiritual practices of sages who are believed to have performed penance and worshipped Lord Shiva here. The presence of Shukla Tirtha forms an important part of the spiritual identity of Bilinele.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter">
        <figure class="chapter-media">
          <img src="/gallery/temple-close.png" alt="The stone shrine." data-i18n="gallery.close" data-i18n-attr="alt" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption data-i18n="gallery.close">The stone shrine.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 data-i18n="about.lingaTitle">The Jyotirlinga Tradition</h2>
          <p data-i18n="about.jyotirlinga">
            According to tradition, the worship of a sacred Jyotirlinga entrusted to the disciples continued through generations. Over time, it is believed that the Brahmin community of the village, together with other devotees, established a temple at this sacred site, continuing the worship and traditions associated with the kshetra.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-copy">
        <h2 data-i18n="about.placesTitle">Kudurekatte and Horikatte</h2>
        <p data-i18n="about.places1">
          On the road from Bilinele towards Subramanya, two traditional raised platforms can be seen on either side of the road. They are known as Kudurekatte and Horikatte.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.places2">
          According to local tradition, these places are associated with the cattle-rearing practices of earlier times and stand as reminders of the important role that cattle and agriculture have played in the life of the surrounding communities.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-copy">
        <h2 data-i18n="about.goTitle">A kshetra associated with Go Seva and Go Durita Nivarana</h2>
        <p data-i18n="about.go1">
          Sri Gopalakrishna Kshetra holds a special place in the devotional traditions of the region for prayers and seva associated with the well-being of cattle.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.go2">
          The tradition of Go Durita Nivarana is associated with the kshetra, through which devotees seek divine grace during difficulties involving cattle and agriculture. According to local belief, devotees have traditionally visited the temple and undertaken vows and seva when cattle go missing, when cattle face danger from wild animals, or when agricultural activities are affected by difficulties.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.go3">
          Devotees also seek the blessings of Sri Gopalakrishna for various family and personal concerns, including Santana Prapti (blessings of children), marital well-being, harmony within the family and relief from difficulties.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.go4">
          The sacred Santana Gopalakrishna Vrata is observed by devotees seeking the blessings of Sri Bala Gopala for children and family well-being.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.go5">
          Many devotees return to the temple after their prayers or vows have been fulfilled and offer seva as an expression of gratitude.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter flip">
        <figure class="chapter-media">
          <img src="/gopalakrishna.png" alt="Sri Gopalakrishna, the presiding deity, worshipped as Bala Gopala." data-i18n="gallery.deity" data-i18n-attr="alt" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption data-i18n="gallery.deity">Sri Gopalakrishna, the presiding deity, worshipped as Bala Gopala.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h2 data-i18n="deity.title">Sri Bala Gopala</h2>
          <p data-i18n="about.deityLead">
            The presiding deity of the temple is Sri Gopalakrishna, worshipped in the divine form of Bala Gopala.
          </p>
          <p data-i18n="about.deityForm">
            Sri Bala Gopala represents the child form of Lord Krishna and is lovingly worshipped by devotees seeking divine grace, protection, happiness and prosperity. The devotion to Bala Gopala forms the heart of the spiritual traditions of this kshetra.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-copy">
        <h2 data-i18n="about.shrinesTitle">Other deities</h2>
        <p data-i18n="about.shrines">The temple complex is also home to several other revered deities:</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li data-i18n="about.deity1">Sri Mahaganapati</li>
          <li data-i18n="about.deity2">Sri Shasthara</li>
          <li data-i18n="about.deity3">Sri Kamalamba Devi</li>
        </ul>
        <p data-i18n="about.kamalamba">
          Among these, the shrine of Sri Kamalamba Devi holds special significance during Navaratri. Special worship and religious observances are performed for nine days during the festival.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-copy">
        <h2 data-i18n="about.livingTitle">A living spiritual tradition</h2>
        <p data-i18n="about.living1">
          The history of Sri Gopalakrishna Temple is not only a story of a temple, but also of a sacred landscape, spiritual traditions and generations of devotees who have preserved their faith and customs.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.living2">
          Through daily worship, Go Seva, Vratas, festivals, Homa, Mantra Japa, Bhajans and Annadana, the traditions of this kshetra continue to remain a living part of the community.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="about.living3">
          Surrounded by the natural beauty of Bilinele and enriched by its spiritual heritage, Sri Gopalakrishna Kshetra continues to welcome devotees with the timeless message of faith, devotion and seva.
        </p>
      </section>

